c0011
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Yes.

c0012
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Hello, Esther?

c0013
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Let me tell you the main subject, you know, of tonight's lecture.

c0014
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:22.000
We will be speaking about one of the great men in Jewish history.

c0015
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.000
And that is Maran Yosef Qaro, the author of the Shulhan 'Arukh.

c0016
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Now, before we discuss the life, works, and ideas, and the reactions of my managers.

c0017
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Now, the reason why I want to discuss Maran is because this series of lectures deals with those men.

c0018
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Works and ideas that shaped our personality, our character.

c0019
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.000
What is idiosyncratic about our community, about the way we are.

c0020
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.000
It is not the result of some sort of random coincidences.

c0021
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:25.000
But it's rather, it was the design of some great Hakhamim, who with and through their ideas, helped shape the Sepharadi world.

c0022
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Now, before I go into the substance, one of the things I want to show you tonight,

c0023
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:40.000
is that the so-called Sephardim, the Sepharadi tradition, was born not in Spain.

c0024
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:51.000
But it was born after the expulsion from Spain, and it is really the result of the creation of Maran Akadosh.

c0025
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Now, that is the major thesis.

c0026
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Sephardim, it is a post-Spanish creation, and you're going to see why and how.

c0027
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Now, let me begin with 1492.

c0028
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.000
Jews are expelled from Spain.

c0029
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:21.000
And there were three major things that shook the Jewish people.

c0030
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Number one, Spain, Spanish Jewry, was the wealthiest, the oldest, and the best organized community in Western Europe.

c0031
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:47.000
The expulsion meant that the Jewish institutions, the Jewish leadership, had failed.

c0032
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:55.000
They realized, the people, that something was wrong with the leaders.

c0033
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Of course, there were great Hakhamim, like Rabbi Shelomo Ibn Verga, the author of Shevet Yehuda,

c0034
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.000
who already pointed out, while in Spain, the failure of the leadership.

c0035
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:14.000
And these people believed that the failure of the leadership, the failure of both rabbis and layperson,

c0036
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.000
was the fact that the anti-Mimonidians, with the whole institution, introducing the whole kind of learning,

c0037
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.000
you know, the populistic way of learning, the prohibition of learning, of studying languages,

c0038
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.000
and studying Latin, and learning how to communicate with the goyim, that was the failure.

c0039
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.000
But three things happened.

c0040
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.000
Number one, large numbers of Jews converted. They became Christians.

c0041
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:48.000
And by becoming Christians, in a way, they separated themselves from the families.

c0042
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Families, every single Jewish family, or most of the Jewish families, were wounded.

c0043
00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:02.000
Because, when they had to leave Spain, those who were formerly Christians, had to remain in Spain.

c0044
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Spain, therefore, was a tearing, really, that tore the Jewish family inside, from the inside.

c0045
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.000
They had to leave parents, they left their children, children left their parents,

c0046
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.000
wives left their husbands, husbands left their wives.

c0047
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Let me just tell you a simple incident, one incident out of tens of thousands.

c0048
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.000
The most powerful Jew at the time, was no other than Donizaka Barbanel.

c0049
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.000
Minister of Finance, the wealthiest Jew of the time.

c0050
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Nobody was as wealthy as he was. He had access to the queen, to the king.

c0051
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:59.000
As he was leaving, the nurse of his grandchild, whose name was Shemuel Barbanel, she kidnapped him.

c0052
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.000
She kidnapped him, and then she goes to the church and she converted him.

c0053
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.000
Against the will of the grandfather and the father.

c0054
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Now, this is one example, should we leave the child there?

c0055
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.000
Is he going to grow as a Christian?

c0056
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Shall we stay?

c0057
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.000
The queen and the king lured, or tried to lure Barbanel and said,

c0058
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:34.000
if you stay, we are going to write a special document, in which you are going to be exempted from the inquisition.

c0059
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.000
The inquisition will not be able to touch you, and all your fortune is going to remain with you.

c0060
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.000
Alright? And if not, and then you'll be able to take care of your grandson.

c0061
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.000
You'll be able to be united, the families will be united.

c0062
00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:54.000
And if not, the only thing he, because he offers great services to the kingdom,

c0063
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:59.000
they will let him take a thousand golden coins, all his property, everything confiscated,

c0064
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
and he will have to leave his grandchild.

c0065
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.000
So Barbanel decides, he says,

c0066
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.000
and he follows the people and leaves his grandson.

c0067
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.000
Eventually, later on, his grandson escapes.

c0068
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:18.000
But the grandson was only one year old, when he was kidnapped.

c0069
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.000
That was rampant.

c0070
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.000
So now, you have three things to remember.

c0071
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:35.000
Number one, Jews in Spain realized that their institutions and leadership failed them.

c0072
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.000
Second, many converted.

c0073
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:45.000
You know, let us not judge them, because when you see the atrocities committed against them,

c0074
00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.000
you know, like they say in Arabic,

c0075
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.000
we should never be in a similar situation, because it was really horrible.

c0076
00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:57.000
And because they were converted, they could not follow their brothers and sisters and husbands and wives.

c0077
00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Another example, for instance, somebody converted, became a Christian, and became a bishop.

c0078
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.000
His wife refused to convert.

c0079
00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.000
She leaves, he does not leave.

c0080
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Separation, breaking of the family.

c0081
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.000
And then there was martyrdom.

c0082
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:23.000
Thousands and thousands and thousands of Jews were burned at the stake.

c0083
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.000
So therefore, now you have to imagine, 1492, leaving.

c0084
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.000
It was only three months for people to leave.

c0085
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Remember, we are not in the 1900s.

c0086
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.000
There was no such thing as traveling ships and so on.

c0087
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.000
Everything had to be done.

c0088
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.000
And you have to transfer people to countries that you don't know.

c0089
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.000
There was no such thing as a pamphlet to tell you, I mean, if you are in Spain, you don't know what Syria is.

c0090
00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.000
Where is Syria? Where is it?

c0091
00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.000
Where is Turkey? They don't know none of those things.

c0092
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.000
A few great men organized, in less than three months,

c0093
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:13.000
a trip that took over, between half a million and a million Jews left Spain.

c0094
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:21.000
Now when they left Spain, penniless, penniless,

c0095
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.000
you were not permitted to take any money with you.

c0096
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:31.000
There were a few men, like Ababalen and so on, who managed to take a very small amount.

c0097
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.000
Family is broken, you go to a country that you don't know the language,

c0098
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.000
alright, and you don't know what to do.

c0099
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.000
That was 1492.

c0100
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.000
The worst year in Jewish history prior to the Holocaust.

c0101
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:53.000
A horrible thing.

c0102
00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:02.000
Now, when the Jews come, they go to two major places.

c0103
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.000
One of it is the Ottoman Empire.

c0104
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.000
The Ottoman Empire was what today is called Turkey.

c0105
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:20.000
But at the time, it comprised all the way from Greece, Iraq, Baghdad,

c0106
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:32.000
part of India, part of modern India, and most of Egypt, Syria, Israel, and most of North Africa.

c0107
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.000
The Jews come there.

c0108
00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.000
And when they come there, they discover something.

c0109
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.000
They discover ancient communities.

c0110
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:47.000
Communities that had been around the Mediterranean basin,

c0111
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.000
that Sephardim, all the Sephardim were around the Mediterranean basin,

c0112
00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.000
for thousands of years.

c0113
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.000
Let me talk about Turkey.

c0114
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
Specifically about Istanbul.

c0115
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.000
Which in those days was not called Istanbul.

c0116
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.000
The Jews never called Istanbul Istanbul.

c0117
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Only modern times called Istanbul Istanbul.

c0118
00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.000
Do you know how the Jews called Istanbul?

c0119
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.000
No?

c0120
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:12.000
Costa.

c0121
00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.000
Costa.

c0122
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:16.000
The Jews called it Costa.

c0123
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.000
It is a short for Constantinople.

c0124
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Now, that was the capital of the Empire.

c0125
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.000
And they go there.

c0126
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:28.000
And you know what they discover?

c0127
00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.000
Jews with great learning.

c0128
00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.000
Extraordinary learning.

c0129
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:45.000
Knowledge of Jewish law that in many, many aspects surpassed whatever was in Spain in the last 150 years before the Gerush.

c0130
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:50.000
And those Jews, do you know what language they spoke?

c0131
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.000
No?

c0132
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.000
They spoke Greek.

c0133
00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.000
They spoke Greek.

c0134
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.000
They were Greek speaking Jews.

c0135
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:09.000
And the great Rabbi of the Ottoman Empire at the time was Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrahi.

c0136
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.000
Now Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrahi was a Greek speaking Jew.

c0137
00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:16.000
His language was Greek.

c0138
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:24.000
Because the Greeks, what is today Turkey, at one time was part of the Byzantine Empire.

c0139
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.000
They spoke Greek.

c0140
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:37.000
Now, when they come there, after a short time, the Sephardim, the Spanish, take over.

c0141
00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:41.000
But they take over in a very intelligent way.

c0142
00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:44.000
Let me tell you what were the problems.

c0143
00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:53.000
First of all, in the last 150 years before leaving Spain, thanks to the school of the Gerush and others, no study of Tora was made.

c0144
00:13:54.000 --> 00:14:02.000
So from the moment that the Gerush comes to Spain, all the glory of Spain disappears.

c0145
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:12.000
There is not one single book of Sheolotu Shuvot produced in the last 150 years in Castile.

c0146
00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.000
Not one single commentary to the Talmud.

c0147
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:22.000
The great Rabbi of Spain at the time, Issachar Barbanel, was Portuguese.

c0148
00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:31.000
Arama, the other great Amir Hakan, Arama, he was not from Castile, he was from Saragossa, from a different place.

c0149
00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:33.000
He was from Aragon.

c0150
00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:40.000
The people from Castile, the Gerush, they produced absolutely nothing.

c0151
00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:47.000
When they come there, they start learning how to learn.

c0152
00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:56.000
And those great rabbis, there was Eliahu Kapsali, Rabbi Shalomuach Cohen of Corfu, these were great extraordinary Hakhamim.

c0153
00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:01.000
And they were the masters of Sheolotu Shuvot, of response, of legal literature.

c0154
00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:10.000
So the Sepharadim, the Spanish, start learning from these Hakhamim how to handle Jewish law.

c0155
00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:16.000
And not only they learn, but in a sense later on, they supersede them.

c0156
00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.000
And they integrate in the community.

c0157
00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.000
And there was something very important that happened.

c0158
00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:31.000
When the Sepharadim came to the Ottoman Empire, and I'm going to give you an example from Halab and from Sham in a second.

c0159
00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:41.000
When they came to Spain, Spain was never a, even until today, it was never a cultural uniform.

c0160
00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.000
Every place in Spain has a different language.

c0161
00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:53.000
Catalan, Gallego, Sefarad, there's at least 30 some languages in Spain.

c0162
00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:00.000
Leones, Aragonese, and those communities, although they were Spanish, they had nothing in common.

c0163
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:10.000
So they had to unite among themselves, and also they had to unite with the local communities.

c0164
00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:16.000
And the question was, how do you forge the basis of unity?

c0165
00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:24.000
One speaks Greek, the other speaks Aragonese, the other speaks Arabic, the other speaks Ladino, the other speaks Portuguese.

c0166
00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:31.000
In Istanbul, Mother Istanbul, Costa Rica, there were 43 different communities.

c0167
00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.000
43 communities.

c0168
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:41.000
And every community with its own language, its own philosophy.

c0169
00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:47.000
How do you bring unity? Do you understand? 43 communities.

c0170
00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:52.000
Let me give you, for better, an example of Syria.

c0171
00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:55.000
Jews come from Syria, they come to Syria, alright?

c0172
00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.000
Take Aleppo as an example.

c0173
00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:03.000
Aleppo, there were, first and foremost, there were Mustaharab.

c0174
00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:07.000
People that spoke Arabic, they were there for centuries.

c0175
00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.000
Arabic-speaking Jews.

c0176
00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:16.000
Second, there were Jews that Rabbi Salem spoke about them last week, from Erez Israel.

c0177
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:19.000
They're a different minhag, not from Bavel.

c0178
00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.000
They follow Tammuz Yerushalmi.

c0179
00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.000
Those are the people who say Asheret, instead of Asheret.

c0180
00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:31.000
Alright? They make conditions with tabba'a, with the matbea, not with the rain.

c0181
00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:34.000
Alright? These were people from Erez Israel.

c0182
00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:41.000
Second, then they had people who came from Castile, who came from Portugal.

c0183
00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:46.000
And there were people who, later on, came from Italy.

c0184
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:51.000
In Damascus, there was more, more things.

c0185
00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:54.000
There was people who were Greek-speaking.

c0186
00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:56.000
Damascus, at one time, spoke Greek.

c0187
00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:58.000
There were Greek-speaking Jews.

c0188
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.000
There were Mustaharab.

c0189
00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:02.000
Then there were Portuguese, Spanish.

c0190
00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.000
And then, there was Jerusalem, Sicily.

c0191
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.000
In 1596, expelled the Jews, they come there.

c0192
00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:18.000
Alright? So then you have a mixture of different communities, different languages, and different outlooks.

c0193
00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.000
Alright? I want you to understand the situation.

c0194
00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:28.000
How do you forge unity among this type of people?

c0195
00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:30.000
Alright? Now.

c0196
00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:32.000
You understand the word? Difficult word.

c0197
00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.000
Now, the result.

c0198
00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:37.000
You know what the result of this, how it is solved?

c0199
00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:39.000
You're going to see how it is solved later on in the lecture.

c0200
00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:43.000
The result is to create something that we call Sefaradi.

c0201
00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:48.000
And Sefaradi is a synthesis of all those cultures.

c0202
00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:56.000
And you're going to see how, who did it with Maran, and how he did it, and what was the result of it.

c0203
00:18:57.000 --> 00:18:59.000
Now. One second.

c0204
00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:01.000
You understand the problem?

c0205
00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:06.000
When Jews left Spain, 1492.

c0206
00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.000
There was a young man.

c0207
00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:12.000
He was only 18 years old at the time.

c0208
00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:14.000
Rabbi Akoberav.

c0209
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:23.000
Rabbi Akoberav was regarded as the most learned Talmud Hakam of the time.

c0210
00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:25.000
Alright?

c0211
00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.000
Just to give you an example.

c0212
00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.000
This is something.

c0213
00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:34.000
If you study one page of Talmud, then you'll appreciate what I'm going to say now.

c0214
00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:41.000
They say that the Talmud has six divisions.

c0215
00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.000
Three divisions, he knew it word by word.

c0216
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:49.000
The other three, I'm sorry, three divisions he knew word by word.

c0217
00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.000
The other three divisions he had in his small pocket.

c0218
00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:53.000
He also knew it word by word.

c0219
00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:55.000
That was at the age of 18.

c0220
00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:57.000
What a genius.

c0221
00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:58.000
Now.

c0222
00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:00.000
He was a genius.

c0223
00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:07.000
And when he left Spain, one of the largest communities is established in North Africa.

c0224
00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:09.000
In Morocco.

c0225
00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:11.000
In a city called Fes.

c0226
00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:15.000
And that community had 5,000 families.

c0227
00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:19.000
That's what I would consider an enormous community.

c0228
00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:25.000
By contrast, in Damascus, that also was a large community, they had 500 families.

c0229
00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:28.000
In Halab, in those days, they had 300 families.

c0230
00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:33.000
So, to have 5,000 families, it was an enormous, enormous.

c0231
00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.000
And he came out with an idea.

c0232
00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:39.000
He wants to establish unity.

c0233
00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.000
You know what he wants to do?

c0234
00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.000
He wants to have a Sanhedrin.

c0235
00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.000
A Jewish Senate.

c0236
00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:52.000
With the same authority as the Sanhedrin.

c0237
00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.000
Sanhedrin.

c0238
00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:55.000
In the time of Rabbeinu Akkadosh.

c0239
00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.000
In the time of the Temple.

c0240
00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.000
How do you do that?

c0241
00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.000
Well, because he realized.

c0242
00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.000
I'll tell you the reasons why he did it.

c0243
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:08.000
He wanted to do it.

c0244
00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.000
He realized that the times have changed.

c0245
00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.000
You know, imagine.

c0246
00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:17.000
This is talking about the year 1530 already.

c0247
00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:19.000
Yes?

c0248
00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:20.000
He realized that the times have changed.

c0249
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:26.000
That we have to, if we want to unify the Jewish people, we need a central authority.

c0250
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:32.000
And the Hakhamim, they have to become real legislators.

c0251
00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:39.000
And if you are a Sanhedrin, in a sense, you could supersede the Talmud.

c0252
00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:43.000
Things which do not fit, you could make them fit.

c0253
00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.000
Now, how do you do that?

c0254
00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:48.000
Very interesting.

c0255
00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:50.000
Rambam said.

c0256
00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:56.000
One of the requirements to be a Sanhedrin, to be a member of the Sanhedrin, you have to have Semicha.

c0257
00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.000
You have to have an ordination.

c0258
00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.000
Now, how do you get ordained in Judaism?

c0259
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:05.000
There are two ways how to get ordained.

c0260
00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:12.000
One, if you are ordained, by somebody who was ordained, by somebody who was ordained, all the way to Moshe Rabbenu.

c0261
00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.000
Uninterrupted chain to Moshe Rabbenu.

c0262
00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.000
Moshe Rabbenu.

c0263
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:22.000
The last person who had this kind of ordination was Rabbi Asher, the author of the Talmud.

c0264
00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.000
That's one way to do it.

c0265
00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:38.000
Another way to do it is, if all the Rabbis, or the majority of the Rabbis of Israel, will designate somebody as a Musmach.

c0266
00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:46.000
If they designate him as a Musmach, then this person has Semicha.

c0267
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:52.000
And if he has Semicha, then you could form, in theory, a Sanhedrin.

c0268
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:58.000
And this Sanhedrin, it is an equal footing, from the point of view of Jewish prudence.

c0269
00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:03.000
I'm not talking about knowledge and unknowledge, of course it is, with any other Sanhedrin in the world.

c0270
00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:05.000
Alright?

c0271
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:11.000
Rabbi Yaaqob Berav, invoking Rambam, yes?

c0272
00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:19.000
He goes to the largest Jewish city of learning in the world, at the time.

c0273
00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:24.000
It was Safed, not Jerusalem, Safed.

c0274
00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:35.000
Safed was the center of Jewish learning, the great Mekubalim, the great Halachis, the great Talmudis, they were there.

c0275
00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:42.000
And there, he gets 25 Rabbis of Safed, the greatest Rabbis of Safed.

c0276
00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:46.000
They appointed him as Musmach.

c0277
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:53.000
Once he is appointed as Musmach, he appoints four people as Musmachim.

c0278
00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:58.000
One of them is Maram, yes, Maram Qadosh, he was a Musmach.

c0279
00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:02.000
But here has come now the tragedy of Judaism.

c0280
00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:05.000
The tragedy of Judaism.

c0281
00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:12.000
A great Talmud Hakham at the time, Rabbi Levi ben Habib, from Jerusalem, he opposed him.

c0282
00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:20.000
Eventually, the things are heard by the Ottoman government.

c0283
00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:30.000
Somebody tells him, this Rabbi, Rabbi Yaaqob Berav, he is instituting a Jewish government, which is in competition with your government.

c0284
00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:36.000
And anybody who has anything to do with Turkey, if you know Turkey, anything like this, like my grandfather used to tell me.

c0285
00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:40.000
The least punishment that they would do to you is chop your head.

c0286
00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:50.000
So, the Turks got a hold of Rabbi Yaaqob Berav.

c0287
00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:56.000
He was fortunate and he escaped and he settled and died in Damascus.

c0288
00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:00.000
That was one attempt.

c0289
00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:07.000
The attempt to unify the Jewish people by establishing a Sanhedrin.

c0290
00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:18.000
Now, I tell you a parenthesis and then I go into Maram Qadosh, because you have to understand his background before you read about him.

c0291
00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:30.000
Rabbi Yaaqob Berav's attempt wanted to be renewed by the first two Rabbis of modern Israel.

c0292
00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:33.000
And by the way, they were great Rabbis.

c0293
00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:36.000
I was fortunate and honored to know both of them.

c0294
00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:44.000
Rabbi Oziel, the Sepharadi Rabbi, an extraordinary Rabbi.

c0295
00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:53.000
And Rabbi Isaac Herzog of the Ashkenaz, the father of the President of Israel.

c0296
00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:02.000
The President of Israel is the son of Rabbi Isaac Herzog, who was an angel.

c0297
00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:12.000
They wanted to re-establish the Sanhedrin in the same way as Rabbi Yaaqob Berav.

c0298
00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:18.000
And there was a man who was the first Jew to be Minister of Religion.

c0299
00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:23.000
He changed his name, he loved so much Rambam, that he changed his name to Maimon.

c0300
00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:28.000
I think his real name was Fischer, Yaaqob Fischer, but he changed it to Yaaqob Maimon.

c0301
00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:31.000
And he wanted very, very much to do it.

c0302
00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:42.000
Unfortunately, there is no unity and the efforts of these three Rabbis did not mature.

c0303
00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:49.000
Many of the questions of today, dividing Jews, this is permitted, that is permitted.

c0304
00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:54.000
Divorce, not divorce, this, that, this, two days of Galilee, one day of Yom Tob.

c0305
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:03.000
All this could be resolved because you have an authorized body that can exactly tell you what the law is.

c0306
00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:12.000
I would be in a hope that this is still one of my dreams.

c0307
00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:20.000
I hope one day there is enough unity among the Jewish people to try to do what Rabbi Yaaqob Berav attempted.

c0308
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:26.000
And unfortunately, as I said, it was a torpedo because of internal Jewish dissent.

c0309
00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:40.000
Now that you have the background, let's go to a little boy who lived in Spain, 1492, and he was already four years old.

c0310
00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:43.000
All right, he was four years old.

c0311
00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:48.000
Now, this boy was Rabbi Yosef Qaro.

c0312
00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.000
He lived in Spain. He was born in Toledo.

c0313
00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:59.000
And he settles down in a city called Adrianople.

c0314
00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.000
It's called, among the Jews, it's called Idrani.

c0315
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.000
I don't know if you know Idrani.

c0316
00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:09.000
It's interesting, Jews, we did not call the cities by the name of the goyim.

c0317
00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:11.000
We had our own names.

c0318
00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:14.000
So the Jews, Adrianople, we called it Idrani.

c0319
00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.000
So he was educated there.

c0320
00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:23.000
And after a short time, his father, Rabbi Ephraim, dies.

c0321
00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:32.000
And he is raised and educated by one of the most illustrious men of the time, Rabbi Hakkaro.

c0322
00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:40.000
From Idrani, he goes to Kosta, Istanbul.

c0323
00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:50.000
And then eventually to one of the most important cities in Jewish history, in modern Jewish history, was Thessaloniki.

c0324
00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:56.000
Thessaloniki was the greatest city in the Jewish world.

c0325
00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:00.000
So much so, I want to tell you something, so much so, Thessaloniki,

c0326
00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:06.000
that the great historian, Cesar Roth, talking about the Second World War,

c0327
00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:10.000
he says, from all the catastrophe of the Second World War,

c0328
00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:17.000
the worst thing that the Germans did, I mean, that harmed the Jewish people, was the destruction of Thessaloniki.

c0329
00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:19.000
By the way, Thessaloniki was destroyed.

c0330
00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:25.000
One of the persons involved in the destruction and deportation of the Jews from Thessaloniki

c0331
00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:30.000
was the distinguished Secretary of the United Nations and the President of Austria.

c0332
00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:32.000
What's his name? Waldheim.

c0333
00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.000
Yes, and he was rewarded.

c0334
00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:40.000
Whoever believes that the United Nations did not know that Waldheim was involved,

c0335
00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:45.000
becomes Secretary of the United Nations without checking your background.

c0336
00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:50.000
I mean, if you believe in this, I have several bridges here that I'm ready to sell.

c0337
00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:54.000
Alright? Now, this is ridiculous, simply ridiculous.

c0338
00:29:54.000 --> 00:30:01.000
Alright, now, he went to Thessaloniki, and there he meets the great Hakhamim,

c0339
00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:09.000
both from the Greek-speaking communities and also those who came from Sefarad, the old-timers.

c0340
00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:11.000
There were a few old-timers.

c0341
00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:18.000
As all Sepharadim, Yosef Qaro supported himself through commerce.

c0342
00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:19.000
He was a businessman.

c0343
00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:24.000
But his ambition was to come to Erez Israel.

c0344
00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.000
Now, I'm going to tell a story.

c0345
00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:29.000
You don't have to believe it.

c0346
00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.000
It's just a story.

c0347
00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:38.000
But as fantastic as the story is, at least to me it sounds fantastic,

c0348
00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:45.000
if you don't believe in that story, it's very difficult to explain the life of Marani Yosef Qaro.

c0349
00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.000
The story goes as follows.

c0350
00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:50.000
You know, you've never been in a souk.

c0351
00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:54.000
You know where the souk is, like when you go to Jerusalem, in Jerusalem,

c0352
00:30:55.000 --> 00:31:00.000
he had a bosta, a partner in the souk.

c0353
00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:05.000
You know, they were selling altezachen probably, you know, old things and so on.

c0354
00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.000
That's what they were selling.

c0355
00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:12.000
So one day, Marani Yosef Qaro comes late.

c0356
00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:16.000
Marani Yosef Qaro comes late to the store.

c0357
00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:22.000
The partner was very nice, and he realized that he came late because he was studying Tora.

c0358
00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.000
And he didn't like it. He didn't want to have this kind of partner.

c0359
00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.000
He says, listen, let's break the partnership.

c0360
00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:31.000
So Marani says, all right.

c0361
00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:37.000
Let's see a balance, how much we have here.

c0362
00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:44.000
So he says, ten liras.

c0363
00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:48.000
So give him five, that's yours.

c0364
00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:59.000
As he's going home, he sees a tall, thin man with a big mortar.

c0365
00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:02.000
You know what a mortar is, yes?

c0366
00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:10.000
You use it to crush things.

c0367
00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.000
A big one, a big mortar, yes?

c0368
00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:19.000
To crush pills, everything, nuts, whatever it is.

c0369
00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.000
They crush it with a mortar.

c0370
00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:29.000
All right, now the guy says to Marani,

c0371
00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:33.000
he says, I bought this thing, I want to sell it.

c0372
00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:37.000
So Marani says, how much do you want for this?

c0373
00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:40.000
So the guy says, how much do you have?

c0374
00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:44.000
So he says, I have five liras.

c0375
00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:48.000
So he says, all right, give me five liras, I'll give you...

c0376
00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:52.000
It was a big, heavy mortar, you see?

c0377
00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:58.000
He goes to his house, tells the wife the story,

c0378
00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:04.000
and says, I'm going to try to sell the mortar.

c0379
00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:08.000
And then he goes to relax a little bit.

c0380
00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:12.000
Now, as he's relaxing, his wife looks into the mortar

c0381
00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:16.000
and sees this strange metal, and she never saw such a metal.

c0382
00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:20.000
So he goes to the next door neighbor and says,

c0383
00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:25.000
look at this mortar, what is it made of?

c0384
00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.000
He says, this is made of gold.

c0385
00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.000
This is made of gold.

c0386
00:33:31.000 --> 00:33:32.000
Gold.

c0387
00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:39.000
Now, she wakes up Marani, and the guy tells him, this is gold.

c0388
00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:44.000
The guy was a professional goldsmith, a sire,

c0389
00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:48.000
and they put it in some sort of an acid, they pour it, and it's gold.

c0390
00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:50.000
So Marani had a problem.

c0391
00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:52.000
What's the problem?

c0392
00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:57.000
According to Jewish law, just like you're not permitted to sell something for something else,

c0393
00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:00.000
you're not permitted to sell, I don't know, copper for gold,

c0394
00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:05.000
the same way somebody sells you gold thinking that it's copper,

c0395
00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:08.000
it's haram to buy from him, it's called ona'a.

c0396
00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:12.000
So he wants to find out about this man.

c0397
00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:17.000
The man looks, asks all over, they never saw him.

c0398
00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:22.000
And then, that night, he has a dream,

c0399
00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:25.000
the man comes to him and says,

c0400
00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.000
B'olam knows that your heart is, you want to study at Yisrael,

c0401
00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:32.000
and you don't want to be supported by anybody,

c0402
00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:34.000
this is for you.

c0403
00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:37.000
And by the way, he goes to Yisrael,

c0404
00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:40.000
he lived to the age of 87,

c0405
00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:43.000
he never took a penny from anybody,

c0406
00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:46.000
and he published his major works,

c0407
00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:49.000
all of it he published from his own pocket.

c0408
00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:52.000
So, how did he get the money?

c0409
00:34:53.000 --> 00:34:54.000
Yes?

c0410
00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:57.000
There was no horses, there they are, there was no roulette,

c0411
00:34:58.000 --> 00:34:59.000
there was no lottery,

c0412
00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:03.000
the only story, I know the story,

c0413
00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:05.000
and I have no other explanation,

c0414
00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:08.000
what else can I tell you, this is the only explanation I have.

c0415
00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:09.000
Alright?

c0416
00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:12.000
El Haslo, yes?

c0417
00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:14.000
He goes to Yisrael,

c0418
00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:16.000
and he goes to Safed.

c0419
00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:18.000
He goes to Safed.

c0420
00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:23.000
There, as I said, he is ordained by Rabbi Yaaqob Berav,

c0421
00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:25.000
and in Safed,

c0422
00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:29.000
they had what was the nucleus of Jewish studies.

c0423
00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:32.000
The great Mekubalim, qabbala,

c0424
00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:35.000
the great teachers of Talmud,

c0425
00:35:35.000 --> 00:35:38.000
the great halachists were there.

c0426
00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:43.000
And in Safed, there is something which is absolutely unique in the Jewish world.

c0427
00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:47.000
They had one place called Beit Havad.

c0428
00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:54.000
Beit Havad was like an assembly of all the great Hakhamim.

c0429
00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:57.000
Now, what was unique about this?

c0430
00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:01.000
That whenever a question came to Safed,

c0431
00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:06.000
no individual Rabbi was permitted to answer it.

c0432
00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:09.000
What you have to do first, before you answer it,

c0433
00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:13.000
you bring this question to the Va'at,

c0434
00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:17.000
you let everybody examine it,

c0435
00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:19.000
you discuss it,

c0436
00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:24.000
and then, an answer is issued.

c0437
00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:32.000
I believe that one of the great intellectual experiences that happened to Maran

c0438
00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:39.000
was the fact that he had there to discuss law

c0439
00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:43.000
with simply the greatest Hakhamim of the time.

c0440
00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:48.000
And I want to mention one in particular who outlived Maran.

c0441
00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:54.000
And he was an extraordinary Hakhamim, also ordained by Rabbi Yaaqob Berav,

c0442
00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:58.000
and his name was Mabit, Moshe Ben Yosef Ditrani.

c0443
00:36:59.000 --> 00:37:01.000
A family of Ditrani, he was an extraordinary Hakhamim,

c0444
00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:06.000
and there is always arguments between Maran and him.

c0445
00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:11.000
Now, Maran, no question about this,

c0446
00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:16.000
in matters of learning, of erudition, of scholarship,

c0447
00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:20.000
he was the greatest Jew since Rambam.

c0448
00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:29.000
Nobody, nobody was near his learning,

c0449
00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.000
his critical abilities,

c0450
00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:37.000
his erudition, mastery of all the complete,

c0451
00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:40.000
everything in Judaism.

c0452
00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:45.000
He was one of those exceptional human beings.

c0453
00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:51.000
But above all, he had one aim in life.

c0454
00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:55.000
The aim, as he said in the introduction to the Bet Yosef,

c0455
00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:58.000
is Tora Ahad.

c0456
00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:03.000
He wants to have one single law for all the Jewish people.

c0457
00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:08.000
He wants to unite the Jewish people and make it into one.

c0458
00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:15.000
Now, his teacher tried to do that, and he failed.

c0459
00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:19.000
How could you do it?

c0460
00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:24.000
Maran comes out, comes with an extraordinary vision.

c0461
00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:34.000
At the time, the main stream of Judaism was divided into three.

c0462
00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:40.000
There was the Ashkenaz, that was one.

c0463
00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:46.000
Then there were the Sepharadim proper, those who came from Spain.

c0464
00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:50.000
And then there were, we have different words for them,

c0465
00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:53.000
Musta'arabim, Toshabim, the local Jews.

c0466
00:38:54.000 --> 00:39:05.000
Now, there were three rabbis that these Jews respected.

c0467
00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:10.000
For the Ashkenaz, the greatest posse was the Rosh.

c0468
00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:12.000
It was the greatest posse.

c0469
00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:15.000
It was the greatest legal mind produced by the Ashkenaz world,

c0470
00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:19.000
in the Middle Ages, was the Rosh. That was one.

c0471
00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:24.000
Then the people, the Musta'arabim, and many people in North Africa,

c0472
00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:29.000
there was another Jew, Rabbi Ishaq al-Fasih, Darwish.

c0473
00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:40.000
And the Jews in the south of Spain, and in the Middle East, Rambam.

c0474
00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:43.000
So he comes up with a fantastic idea.

c0475
00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:47.000
Let's establish Abeddin.

c0476
00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:56.000
But not of living people, because Jews are not going to accept any person who is alive.

c0477
00:39:56.000 --> 00:39:57.000
They are not going to accept him.

c0478
00:39:57.000 --> 00:40:00.000
I don't know whether we would have accepted Musta'arabim if he would have been alive.

c0479
00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.000
I think that was one of the reasons why Boleh Olam in his infinite wisdom says,

c0480
00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:08.000
you know, remove yourself from here.

c0481
00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:13.000
So he takes these three rabbis,

c0482
00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:20.000
and he establishes a theoretic Abeddin, and I'll tell you exactly how he does it.

c0483
00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:21.000
A theoretic Abeddin.

c0484
00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:29.000
The Abeddin is composed of Rosh, Rih, and Rambam.

c0485
00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:32.000
Now you would say, so what?

c0486
00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:34.000
Why should I listen to this Abeddin?

c0487
00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:36.000
Oh, one minute.

c0488
00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:40.000
I, means Yosef Qaro, I am a Musmah.

c0489
00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:48.000
Now in quality of Musmah, I'm going to render a decision.

c0490
00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:56.000
Not based on my views, but based on their views.

c0491
00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:00.000
How are we going to do it? Very simple.

c0492
00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:03.000
We're going to go issue by issue.

c0493
00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:06.000
If everybody agrees, there is no problem.

c0494
00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:09.000
I just follow, I write the decision.

c0495
00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:14.000
I'm a Musmah, yes, and I'm writing a decision.

c0496
00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:16.000
I write the decision, everybody agrees.

c0497
00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:23.000
If there is disagreement, then we follow two out of three.

c0498
00:41:25.000 --> 00:41:29.000
If there is total disagreement, none of the three agree,

c0499
00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:35.000
then I, means Maran, in quality of Musmah,

c0500
00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:43.000
I will determine whose opinion is the most authoritative one.

c0501
00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:49.000
Now, he did it in the following way.

c0502
00:41:50.000 --> 00:41:53.000
The first book that he wrote,

c0503
00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:59.000
it composed eight volumes of the Turk Beyt Yosef.

c0504
00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:03.000
I have the first edition, which is very, very rare.

c0505
00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:05.000
Big like this, thick like this.

c0506
00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:08.000
Eight volumes.

c0507
00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:10.000
Eight volumes.

c0508
00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:15.000
Covering all the laws, not like Al-Anbam,

c0509
00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:21.000
all the laws which are applicable to the pre-Messianic age, to our age.

c0510
00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:24.000
Things to do with Korbanot, with Tumar,

c0511
00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:27.000
other things he does not deal with it, because, you know.

c0512
00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:30.000
Now, here comes something very important.

c0513
00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:34.000
In this book, the Beyt Yosef,

c0514
00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:40.000
he discusses these three opinions,

c0515
00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:44.000
but he does it for the first time in Jewish intellectual history,

c0516
00:42:44.000 --> 00:42:46.000
does it in a critical way.

c0517
00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:52.000
Doesn't say, I like this opinion, this opinion is good, this opinion is bad.

c0518
00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:53.000
No.

c0519
00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:55.000
He looks into the sources,

c0520
00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:01.000
he looks into the different versions, ancient manuscripts, different versions,

c0521
00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:06.000
and on the basis of the Talmud,

c0522
00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:13.000
critically, he shows you which opinion is right,

c0523
00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:17.000
and which opinion missed the point.

c0524
00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:24.000
So Maran is not only authoritatively correct, because I am Usmach,

c0525
00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:34.000
but he is intellectually shows you how to analyze law in light of the Talmud.

c0526
00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:42.000
And in doing this, he establishes a new way how to study Halakha, Jewish law.

c0527
00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:48.000
So, critically, he is not, again, forgive me for repeating the famous sentence,

c0528
00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:51.000
he is not Al-Sheikh, I say so.

c0529
00:43:52.000 --> 00:43:58.000
He shows you, by studying the sources, in a very meticulous way,

c0530
00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:05.000
why one opinion must be discarded, and the other has to be taken seriously.

c0531
00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:13.000
It is the introduction, for the first time, of a critical approach to Rabbinic jurisprudence.

c0532
00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:24.000
And by doing this, Maran changed the way we study Halakha.

c0533
00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:30.000
Briefly, if you look into any of the Sheykhs, prior to Maran,

c0534
00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:36.000
somebody asks him a question, a Rabbi says, in the Gemara, says so and so.

c0535
00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:39.000
And therefore, the decision is X.

c0536
00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:43.000
When they do this, the Rabbis may be correct,

c0537
00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:47.000
but they don't take into consideration the opposite views.

c0538
00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:51.000
There are people who disagree with you.

c0539
00:44:52.000 --> 00:44:57.000
And what Maran is teaching us is, you cannot just tell me what you believe in.

c0540
00:44:57.000 --> 00:45:01.000
You have to show me why you don't believe in the other person's opinion.

c0541
00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:04.000
You understand what I'm trying to say?

c0542
00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:12.000
You have to show that the other opinions, for whatever reason, are incorrect.

c0543
00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:19.000
That was a revolution in Jewish studies.

c0544
00:45:20.000 --> 00:45:30.000
By doing that, Safed, we didn't say Safed, we said Safad.

c0545
00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:38.000
Safad became the most important place because it was Maran there.

c0546
00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:45.000
And they say that at the time, Maran had 200 students.

c0547
00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:49.000
Those days were fabulous.

c0548
00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:57.000
200 students who studied at his feet and learned how to make Pesah halakha.

c0549
00:45:58.000 --> 00:46:02.000
By the way, Maran gave Semihah.

c0550
00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:04.000
He received Semihah.

c0551
00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:10.000
Among the people that received Semihah, I'm just going to mention, I mentioned many of them.

c0552
00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:12.000
Rabbi Moshe Cordovero.

c0553
00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:13.000
I don't know if you heard of him.

c0554
00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:16.000
Rabbi Moshe Cordovero was the greatest Mekubal of the time.

c0555
00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:18.000
Even greater than Ha'ari.

c0556
00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:21.000
Once that he died, then Ha'ari rose up.

c0557
00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:24.000
Rabbi Moshe Cordovero was the greatest Mekubal of the time.

c0558
00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:28.000
Rabbi Moshe Elsheikh, the great Darshan.

c0559
00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:37.000
There was also Rabbi Abraham Laniado, the father-in-law of Rabbi Shemuel Laniado.

c0560
00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:41.000
And Rabbi Shemuel Laniado also got Semihah from Maran HaKalosh.

c0561
00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:44.000
I'm just going to mention a few of them.

c0562
00:46:52.000 --> 00:46:54.000
You have to be a scholar. You have to be a first-rate scholar.

c0563
00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:58.000
As a matter of fact, when I say to my students,

c0564
00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:00.000
first thing after that, bet yourself.

c0565
00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:02.000
This is the most important thing.

c0566
00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:04.000
Study, bet yourself.

c0567
00:47:05.000 --> 00:47:10.000
You people don't know, but the older, the businessmen,

c0568
00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:13.000
all the businessmen used to try to bet yourself Hosham Mishpat.

c0569
00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:16.000
Why? Because all the business, I'm going to tell you later on,

c0570
00:47:17.000 --> 00:47:18.000
was done according to Hosham Mishpat.

c0571
00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:21.000
I'll explain it to you later on.

c0572
00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:23.000
You have to be a scholar.

c0573
00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:26.000
Not everybody could be a scholar.

c0574
00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:31.000
Now he did something else.

c0575
00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:33.000
He wrote the Shulhan 'Arukh.

c0576
00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:36.000
What is the Shulhan 'Arukh?

c0577
00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:37.000
What is the Shulhan 'Arukh?

c0578
00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:42.000
The Shulhan 'Arukh is, very briefly,

c0579
00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:48.000
is a summary of the conclusions of the bet yourself.

c0580
00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:53.000
So now you have two sets of books.

c0581
00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:57.000
When we were children, say in Argentina,

c0582
00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:00.000
in the Syrian community of Argentina,

c0583
00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:03.000
as children, we started, first thing we started, Shulhan 'Arukh.

c0584
00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:06.000
Because Shulhan 'Arukh tells you what the law is.

c0585
00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:10.000
If you want more, because you have questions,

c0586
00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:12.000
how does he say this, there's a question from here,

c0587
00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:14.000
maybe he's wrong, maybe he's right,

c0588
00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:16.000
then you go into the bet yourself.

c0589
00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:21.000
Now, in a short time,

c0590
00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:24.000
Sepharadim all over the world

c0591
00:48:25.000 --> 00:48:31.000
accept the Shulhan 'Arukh as the ultimate law.

c0592
00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:33.000
This becomes the law.

c0593
00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:39.000
There is a story, I happen to believe that it's true.

c0594
00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:41.000
Many haqqanim happen to believe it's true.

c0595
00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:43.000
Some people say, no, it's not true.

c0596
00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:45.000
But just let me tell you what the story says.

c0597
00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:50.000
The story goes that when Maran published the Shulhan 'Arukh,

c0598
00:48:51.000 --> 00:48:54.000
there was a convention of 200 rabbis.

c0599
00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:58.000
And 200 rabbis read the Shulhan 'Arukh

c0600
00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:02.000
and approved of the Shulhan 'Arukh.

c0601
00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:07.000
So, as the Hida, as the one of the haqqanim say,

c0602
00:49:08.000 --> 00:49:09.000
when you disagree with the Shulhan 'Arukh,

c0603
00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:11.000
you're not only disagreeing with Maran,

c0604
00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:15.000
you're disagreeing with the 200 rabbis

c0605
00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:19.000
who approved of Maran.

c0606
00:49:20.000 --> 00:49:21.000
So what happened?

c0607
00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:24.000
In a short time,

c0608
00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:31.000
all the Jewish communities where Sepharadim had come,

c0609
00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:40.000
Aleppo, Damascus, Cairo, Alexandria,

c0610
00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:48.000
Fez, Casablanca, Meknes, Livorno, Venice,

c0611
00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:52.000
Thessaloniki, Kushta,

c0612
00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:54.000
What?

c0613
00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:57.000
Smyrna, of course, I forgot Smyrna.

c0614
00:49:58.000 --> 00:50:05.000
All those great cities became united

c0615
00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:11.000
by having a common law

c0616
00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:16.000
that all accepted as ultimate and binding.

c0617
00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:19.000
So therefore, there were two elements.

c0618
00:50:20.000 --> 00:50:24.000
On the one hand, there was what Maran produced.

c0619
00:50:25.000 --> 00:50:28.000
In a sense, he creates the Shulhan 'Arukh.

c0620
00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:32.000
But the Shulhan 'Arukh was not imposed.

c0621
00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:36.000
But the public accepted it.

c0622
00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:40.000
In my own days, as a child,

c0623
00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:44.000
you mention the word Maran, that's the end of the discussion.

c0624
00:50:44.000 --> 00:50:49.000
Shulhan 'Arukh was the highest law.

c0625
00:50:50.000 --> 00:50:53.000
Nobody ever questioned or disagreed.

c0626
00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:55.000
That was the law.

c0627
00:50:56.000 --> 00:50:58.000
Now, in what way was this good for the Jews?

c0628
00:50:59.000 --> 00:51:03.000
About 20 some years ago,

c0629
00:51:04.000 --> 00:51:10.000
I was invited to give a lecture at Columbia University Law School.

c0630
00:51:11.000 --> 00:51:17.000
And the subject that I chose was the Shulhan 'Arukh

c0631
00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:22.000
and private international trading.

c0632
00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:29.000
Now, I want to tell you how this revolutionized the world.

c0633
00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:36.000
Suppose, at the time, a French goy,

c0634
00:51:37.000 --> 00:51:39.000
a goy from France,

c0635
00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:47.000
wants to buy a species, some spice,

c0636
00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:51.000
say, from India.

c0637
00:51:55.000 --> 00:52:02.000
Now, he needs ships to bring the spice,

c0638
00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:06.000
somehow to the Red Sea, yes,

c0639
00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:10.000
and to bring it somehow to the Mediterranean,

c0640
00:52:11.000 --> 00:52:14.000
and then to Europe.

c0641
00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:18.000
It was impossible for him. Physically impossible. Why?

c0642
00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:22.000
Because when the spices will come to France,

c0643
00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:25.000
first of all, if I send the money to India,

c0644
00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:27.000
I have no means to send the money.

c0645
00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:30.000
How am I going to send the money, not to India, to Italy?

c0646
00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:32.000
How am I going to send the money?

c0647
00:52:32.000 --> 00:52:36.000
What are my legal recourse if I send my money from France to Italy?

c0648
00:52:37.000 --> 00:52:39.000
And the guy receives the money and says,

c0649
00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:41.000
no, I didn't receive it.

c0650
00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:46.000
Now, there was absolutely no legal recourse for you.

c0651
00:52:47.000 --> 00:52:48.000
Or vice versa.

c0652
00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:51.000
Suppose the guy from Italy sends you the merchandise to you.

c0653
00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:59.000
And you say, yes, but it's missing a, I don't know how you say it, a crate.

c0654
00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.000
You have no means to verify it.

c0655
00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:05.000
Suppose something happens on the way.

c0656
00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:09.000
You have no means to insure it.

c0657
00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:13.000
Let me give you an example that I discussed this in my lecture,

c0658
00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:15.000
when I gave my lecture at Columbia.

c0659
00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:17.000
Simple case.

c0660
00:53:20.000 --> 00:53:28.000
A ship that was insured by a Jewish company in Thessaloniki,

c0661
00:53:29.000 --> 00:53:30.000
yes,

c0662
00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:34.000
took merchandise from Venice

c0663
00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:38.000
and in a strip,

c0664
00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:44.000
it harbored in what is called today the Hagues,

c0665
00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:46.000
is near Amsterdam.

c0666
00:53:47.000 --> 00:53:52.000
Now, the ship was insured against fire.

c0667
00:53:53.000 --> 00:53:57.000
Any fire proceeding from the ship,

c0668
00:53:57.000 --> 00:53:59.000
the insurance will pay.

c0669
00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:01.000
Alright?

c0670
00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:08.000
Now, while the ship was at the harbor in the Hague,

c0671
00:54:09.000 --> 00:54:11.000
the harbor caught fire.

c0672
00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:18.000
The fire came from the harbor to the ship.

c0673
00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:23.000
Now, here you have three different countries.

